<section class="section">
  <div class="lede">
    <div class="lede-head">
      <p class="section-num">§ I</p>
      <h2 id="shape">The shape</h2>
    </div>
    <p>Two layouts exist, and which one you have depends on whether the sharded store has been activated.</p>
  </div>
  <p>A fresh install ships the <strong>flat</strong> shape: one file per category, holding many entries, capped by a <code>size-cap:</code> in its frontmatter. Activating <code>memory.sharded_store</code> migrates each of those files into a <strong>sharded</strong> directory of one fact per file. Skills detect the shape from what is on disk rather than by reading the flag, so both are read correctly and the migration is reversible.</p>
  <p>The rest of this page describes the sharded shape, and calls out the flat shape where behaviour differs.</p>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Category</th><th>Holds</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td class="phase">landmarks</td><td>Where things are. A symbol, a file, a line worth finding again.</td></tr>
      <tr><td class="phase">libraries</td><td>Third-party dependencies and the version a decision was pinned to.</td></tr>
      <tr><td class="phase">decisions</td><td>A choice made, its rationale, and what was rejected.</td></tr>
      <tr><td class="phase">landmines</td><td>Something that broke, and how not to break it again.</td></tr>
      <tr><td class="phase">conventions</td><td>How this project does a recurring thing.</td></tr>
      <tr><td class="phase">pending-questions</td><td>Open questions carrying an id, waiting on a human.</td></tr>
      <tr><td class="phase">backlog</td><td>Deferred work with a stable key, closed by a commit that names it.</td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>
  <p>One fact per file means there is no per-file size cap in the sharded shape. A fact never has to be evicted to make room for another one. The 500-line cap belongs to the flat shape, where every fact in a category shares a file and a write that overflows has to prune the oldest unverified entries to fit.</p>
  <p>The seven category files are committed. The three files in the next section are not: staging, continuity, and the thread trail are all gitignored, so nothing half-formed and nothing session-local reaches your teammates.</p>
</section>

<section class="section">
  <div class="lede">
    <div class="lede-head">
      <p class="section-num">§ II</p>
      <h2 id="kinds">Three kinds of memory</h2>
    </div>
    <p>They differ in what they hold and in how long they last.</p>
  </div>
  <p><strong>Project memory</strong> is the seven categories. It accumulates, it is cited, and it is re-verified before use.</p>
  <p><strong>Session memory</strong> is <code>_resume.md</code>, rewritten at the end of every turn and before any compaction. It records what the session was doing, not what the project knows. Reading it is how a fresh session picks up mid-thought.</p>
  <p><strong>The thread trail</strong> is <code>_thread.md</code>. It is local, gitignored, and deliberately outside the flush reset path, so a shelved thread survives both a memory flush and a <code>/clear</code>. Claude shelves and resumes it internally; no skill or command exposes it.</p>
</section>

<section class="section">
  <div class="lede">
    <div class="lede-head">
      <p class="section-num">§ III</p>
      <h2 id="lifecycle">The lifecycle</h2>
    </div>
    <p>Nothing enters project memory without passing through a staging inbox first.</p>
  </div>
  <p>The <code>memory_stop</code> hook runs at the end of every turn and writes candidates into <code>_pending.md</code>. That file is an inbox, not memory. Nothing in it counts until a curation step promotes it.</p>
  <p><code>/memory-flush</code> runs at Phase 10.7 of every committing track, between the roadmap sync and the commit gate. It reviews the candidates, promotes the keepers into canonical files, and resets the pending body.</p>
  <p>Writing straight into a canonical file, outside the natural byproduct of a phase skill, is not allowed. The inbox exists so that memory accrues deliberately rather than as a side effect of a long conversation.</p>
</section>

<section class="section">
  <div class="lede">
    <div class="lede-head">
      <p class="section-num">§ IV</p>
      <h2 id="reverify">Re-verification before citation</h2>
    </div>
    <p>Every skill that cites a memory entry re-verifies it first.</p>
  </div>
  <p>The file still exists, the symbol is still at the named line, the version is still pinned. A failed check is corrected or deleted in the same run, before the citing work continues.</p>
  <div class="callout">
    <span class="icon" aria-hidden="true">!</span>
    <div class="body">
      <strong>This is the rule that keeps the store from becoming confidently wrong.</strong>
      <p>A memory that is never checked is worse than no memory, because it is trusted.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>

<section class="section">
  <div class="lede">
    <div class="lede-head">
      <p class="section-num">§ V</p>
      <h2 id="decay">Decay and closure</h2>
    </div>
    <p>An entry unverified for 30 commits, or 30 days on a project without git, is marked stale.</p>
  </div>
  <p>The session-start hook counts them and reports the total; the next phase that touches one either re-verifies or removes it.</p>
  <p>Closure is a different signal. A backlog entry closed by a commit that named its key carries a closure stamp, and closure short-circuits decay: a closed entry is not stale, it is finished.</p>
</section>

<section class="section">
  <div class="lede">
    <div class="lede-head">
      <p class="section-num">§ VI</p>
      <h2 id="schema">Entry schema</h2>
    </div>
    <p>Frontmatter carries the structured fields, the body carries the fact.</p>
  </div>
  <p><code>key</code>, <code>category</code>, <code>source</code>, <code>verified-at</code>, and <code>last-touched</code> are common to every category. The rest are category-specific: the backlog entry below carries <code>status</code> and <code>raised-on</code> because backlog items are opened and closed, which a landmark never is.</p>
  <pre><code><span class="tok-com">---</span>
<span class="tok-key">key</span>: commit-consent-token-is-never-consumed-after-use
<span class="tok-key">category</span>: backlog
<span class="tok-key">scope</span>: [security, spec, tdd]
<span class="tok-key">status</span>: open
<span class="tok-key">raised-on</span>: 2026-07-25
<span class="tok-key">source</span>: user-instruction
<span class="tok-key">verified-at</span>: ea618e9
<span class="tok-key">last-touched</span>: 2026-07-25
<span class="tok-com">---</span></code></pre>
  <p>Entries sourced from a user instruction or user feedback must quote the user's actual words. When that quote and Claude's interpretation of it disagree, the quote wins and the conflict is surfaced rather than resolved silently. A flush rejects any promotion that should carry a quote and does not.</p>
</section>
